## What a `type: memory` instance is

An entry of the canonical "memorial" type — a markdown document under a corpus tree carrying both YAML frontmatter (structured facts) and a markdown body (narrative content).  Memorials are the foundational unit of a sandbar-backed corpus: they're the persistent representation of a single semantically-coherent unit — a decision, a plan, an observation, a library study, etc.  The `memory-type:` frontmatter field discriminates the kind; the rest of the substrate emerges from per-instance slots.

## When to author a memorial

A corpus memorial is the right unit when:

- The content represents a coherent semantic unit (one decision, one plan, one observation, one library)
- The content benefits from being SEARCHABLE — BM25F across name / description / tags / body
- The content participates in the typed-edge graph — citing other memorials, being cited by others, motivated-by, refines, etc.
- The content has a STABLE IDENTITY that other memorials can reference

Memorials are NOT the right unit when:
- The content is transient or session-scoped (use `memory/inbox/` capture instead)
- The content is a structural component of another memorial (use the body, not a separate file)
- The content fits cleanly into a sub-namespace of an existing memorial (use sections via headings)

